#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""atlas-context — the cold-start packet: what a session needs to resume, and no more.

  atlas context                  the current project, its live tickets, and git state
  atlas context <TICKET-ID>      that ticket in full, plus the same surroundings
  atlas context --json           the same packet, machine-readable
  atlas context --boundary       just the task-boundary reading (see below)
  atlas context --mode planning  the same packet reorganized for planning: project, git
                                  state, each ticket's id/state/title/next action/blockers,
                                  read-next — no objective/verification prose, no log, no
                                  ranking. Mutually exclusive with --boundary and --json:
                                  each is a distinct, non-composable view of the packet.
  atlas context --mode next [--goal G] [--limit N] [--why] [--json]
                                  the Smart Dynamic Ticket System's deterministic
                                  recommendation for the active project: Do now / Owner
                                  decisions / Blocked / Quick wins / Future, from
                                  `atlas_tickets.classify_and_rank` — the same
                                  implementation `atlas tickets next` uses, so the two can
                                  never disagree. Unlike `--mode planning`, this composes
                                  with --json (its output is meant to be ranked and
                                  consumed, not just reorganized evidence).
  atlas context --resume         is a previous session's checkpoint waiting to be picked
                                  up? Reads $ATLAS_HOME/runtime/session-handoffs/latest.md
                                  (generated by `atlas tickets checkpoint`), prints it if
                                  pending, then marks it consumed. No ticket/registry scan,
                                  no git call, no `projects` path resolution at all.
                                  Mutually exclusive with --boundary/--mode; composes with
                                  --json (a real caller — the SessionStart hook adapter —
                                  needs a structured form, not text-scraping).
  atlas context --query Q        (only with the default/--json/--boundary packet, not
                                  --mode/--resume) rank tickets_live by relevance to Q and
                                  record the match in packet["retrieval"] — a retrieval
                                  step, not a filter: every field already computed stays.
  atlas context --budget-chars N (same restriction) compact the JSON packet to fit N
                                  characters, dropping the least essential fields first
                                  (recent_log/artifacts, then repos, then everything but
                                  ids/state/next_action) while keeping every ticket
                                  reconstructable from `record`. Never silently drops a
                                  ticket — only detail per ticket.

## Why a command rather than a document

Resuming used to mean reading a project's `state.md`, `current.md` and `checkpoint.md` and
then the ticket — tens of thousands of tokens, describing a moment that had passed. Three
of those files disagreed with the filesystem the day this was written: the branch, the HEAD
and the test baseline were all stale, because a fact copied into prose stops tracking the
thing it describes.

So the packet is DERIVED, every time, from the records and from git. It cannot be stale,
and it is small enough that reading it is never the expensive part of a task.

## What it deliberately does not include

History. A ticket's log, its verification transcript, its design artifacts and the
project's roadmap and charter are all reachable in one named step from the packet, and none
is admitted into it. The packet says where to look; it does not pre-load the answer.

## The boundary reading

`--boundary` reports the deterministic signals that bear on whether the next request
belongs to the session already running: the project the working directory resolves to, the
tickets that are live, and whether uncommitted work is outstanding. It is deliberately NOT
a verdict. Nothing here can tell from a filesystem whether the user's next sentence is the
same task, and a keyword rule dressed up as semantic judgement would be worse than an
honest gap: it would be trusted. What this gives a caller is the evidence; the reading of
intent stays with whoever has the request in front of them.

## Resume — a generated pointer, not a second source of truth

`atlas tickets checkpoint` is still the one writer of durable ticket state. All it gains
here is one more side effect: after a successful write, it also replaces (never appends
to) `$ATLAS_HOME/runtime/session-handoffs/latest.md` — a small, routing-only pointer
(ticket id, checkpoint time, one-line state/next-action/blockers, the exact command to
read the ticket, and a `pending`/`consumed` marker). `--resume` reads that one file. It
never re-derives it from the ticket, never duplicates the ticket's objective/verification/
log/artifacts, and never executes anything named inside it — it only prints and, once
printed successfully, flips the marker so a second `--resume` does not replay it. A new
checkpoint always overwrites the file, so a fresh checkpoint after consumption is pending
again automatically. `runtime/` is `local`/generated per
`governance/product/atlas-path-classification.yaml` — this file is exactly that: a
transient index, never itself the record.

Zero non-stdlib dependencies.
"""
import argparse, json, os, re, subprocess, sys
from pathlib import Path

sys.path.insert(0, str(Path(__file__).resolve().parent))
from atlas_paths import private_path_or_die, private_path, PathConflict
from atlas_tickets import LIVE, discover, section, first_line, classify_and_rank, render_recommendation

MAX_LOG_LINES = 3
PACKET_SCHEMA = "atlas.context.packet.v1"


def git(repo, *args):
    r = subprocess.run(["git", "-C", str(repo), *args],
                       capture_output=True, text=True)
    return r.stdout.strip() if r.returncode == 0 else ""


def git_state(repo):
    repo = Path(repo)
    if not (repo / ".git").exists():
        top = git(repo, "rev-parse", "--show-toplevel")
        if not top:
            return None
        repo = Path(top)
    dirty = git(repo, "status", "--porcelain")
    return {
        "path": str(repo),
        "branch": git(repo, "rev-parse", "--abbrev-ref", "HEAD"),
        "head": git(repo, "log", "-1", "--format=%h %s"),
        "uncommitted": len([l for l in dirty.splitlines() if l.strip()]),
        "remotes": len([l for l in git(repo, "remote").splitlines() if l.strip()]),
    }


def registry_projects(projects_root):
    """name -> path, from the registry table. Deterministic, no model involved."""
    reg = projects_root / "registry.md"
    out = {}
    if not reg.exists():
        return out
    for line in reg.read_text().splitlines():
        if not line.startswith("|"):
            continue
        cells = [c.strip().strip("`*") for c in line.strip("|").split("|")]
        if len(cells) < 2 or cells[0].lower().startswith(("project", "---")):
            continue
        name, path = cells[0], cells[1]
        if path and path not in ("-", ""):
            out[name] = path
    return out


def active_project(projects_root, cwd):
    """Which project the working directory sits in. Longest matching path wins."""
    cwd = str(Path(cwd).resolve())
    best = None
    for name, path in registry_projects(projects_root).items():
        p = os.path.expanduser(path).rstrip("/")
        if not p:
            continue
        tail = p.split("/")[-1]
        for cand in (p, tail):
            if cand and (f"/{cand}/" in cwd + "/" or cwd.endswith(f"/{cand}")):
                if best is None or len(cand) > best[1]:
                    best = (name, len(cand))
    return best[0] if best else None


def project_dir_for(projects_root, name):
    if not name:
        return None
    slug = re.sub(r"[^a-z0-9]+", "-", name.lower()).strip("-")
    for cand in (projects_root / slug,):
        if cand.is_dir():
            return cand
    for d in sorted(projects_root.iterdir()):
        if d.is_dir() and slug.startswith(d.name):
            return d
    return None


def ticket_packet(t, full):
    body = Path(t["path"]).read_text()
    out = {
        "id": t["id"], "state": t["state"], "title": t["title"],
        "class": t["klass"], "expected_context": t["expected_context"],
        "relation": t.get("relation"), "parent": t.get("parent"),
        "requirement": t.get("requirement"), "goal": t.get("goal"),
        "next_action": t["next_action_line"],
        "record": t["path"],
        # Whether a cold `atlas context <ID>` would carry the work forward on its own.
        # One definition, so `atlas lifecycle` cannot disagree with this packet about
        # whether discarding a context is safe.
        "reconstructs": all(bool((section(body, h) or "").strip())
                            for h in ("Objective", "Next action", "Verification")),
    }
    blockers = section(body, "Blockers")
    if blockers:
        head = first_line(blockers)
        # "None", "None open.", "none — cleared 2026-09-02" all mean there is no blocker.
        # Only a real one earns a line in the packet.
        if head and not re.match(r"(?i)^none\b", head.strip()):
            out["blockers"] = head
    if full:
        out["objective"] = (section(body, "Objective") or "").strip()
        out["next_action"] = (section(body, "Next action") or "").strip()
        ver = section(body, "Verification") or ""
        out["verification"] = ver.strip()
        log = section(body, "Log") or ""
        entries = [l for l in log.splitlines() if l.startswith("- ")]
        out["recent_log"] = entries[-MAX_LOG_LINES:]
        out["artifacts"] = t["artifacts"]
    return out


def _json_chars(value):
    return len(json.dumps(value, ensure_ascii=False, separators=(",", ":")))


def _essential_ticket(t):
    return {k: t[k] for k in ("id", "state", "title", "next_action", "record")
            if k in t}


def retrieve(packet, query):
    """Keep retrieval deterministic: score only fields already present in the packet."""
    terms = [x for x in re.findall(r"[\w-]+", query.lower()) if len(x) > 1]
    if not terms:
        return packet
    candidates = packet.get("tickets_live", [])
    ranked = []
    for ticket in candidates:
        haystack = " ".join(str(ticket.get(k, "")) for k in
                            ("id", "title", "next_action", "objective", "verification"))
        score = sum(haystack.lower().count(term) for term in terms)
        if score:
            ranked.append((score, ticket.get("id", ""), ticket))
    ranked.sort(key=lambda item: (-item[0], item[1]))
    packet["tickets_live"] = [item[2] for item in ranked]
    packet["retrieval"] = {"query": query, "terms": terms,
                            "matches": [item[1] for item in ranked]}
    return packet


def compact_packet(packet, budget_chars):
    """Drop repetition in a fixed order while retaining reconstructable state."""
    if budget_chars is None:
        return packet
    if budget_chars < 512:
        raise ValueError("context packet budget must be at least 512 characters")
    original_chars = _json_chars(packet)
    out = json.loads(json.dumps(packet, ensure_ascii=False))
    out["packet"] = {"schema": PACKET_SCHEMA, "source": "derived",
                     "mode": "compact", "budget_chars": budget_chars,
                     "original_chars": original_chars}

    if _json_chars(out) > budget_chars:
        out["tickets_live"] = [_essential_ticket(t) for t in out.get("tickets_live", [])]
        if out.get("focus"):
            out["focus"] = _essential_ticket(out["focus"])
        out["read_next"] = {"one ticket in full": "atlas context <TICKET-ID>"}
    if _json_chars(out) > budget_chars:
        out["tickets_live"] = [{k: t[k] for k in ("id", "state", "title") if k in t}
                                for t in out.get("tickets_live", [])]
        if out.get("focus"):
            out["focus"] = {k: out["focus"][k] for k in ("id", "state", "title", "next_action")
                             if k in out["focus"]}
        out.pop("repos", None)
    if _json_chars(out) > budget_chars - 128:
        out = {
            "project": out.get("project"),
            "tickets_live": [{k: t[k] for k in ("id", "state") if k in t}
                             for t in out.get("tickets_live", [])],
            "focus": ({k: out["focus"][k] for k in ("id", "state", "next_action")
                       if k in out["focus"]} if out.get("focus") else None),
            "packet": out["packet"],
        }
    out["packet"]["compacted"] = True
    out["packet"]["returned_chars"] = 0
    for _ in range(3):
        out["packet"]["returned_chars"] = _json_chars(out)
    out["packet"]["compacted"] = out["packet"]["returned_chars"] < original_chars
    return out


def build(cwd, ticket_id=None, query=None, budget_chars=None):
    projects_root = private_path_or_die("projects")
    tickets = discover(projects_root)
    proj = active_project(projects_root, cwd)
    pdir = project_dir_for(projects_root, proj)

    live = [t for t in tickets if t["state"] in LIVE]
    if pdir:
        scoped = [t for t in live if t["project_dir"] == pdir.name]
        live = scoped or live

    focus = None
    if ticket_id:
        hit = [t for t in tickets if (t["id"] or "").upper() == ticket_id.upper()]
        if not hit:
            raise SystemExit(f"atlas context: no ticket '{ticket_id}'")
        focus = ticket_packet(hit[0], full=True)

    repos = []
    seen = set()
    for cand in (cwd, os.environ.get("ATLAS_REPO", "") or os.environ.get("ATLAS_REPO", "")):
        if not cand:
            continue
        st = git_state(cand)
        if st and st["path"] not in seen:
            seen.add(st["path"])
            repos.append(st)

    packet = {
        "project": proj,
        "project_dir": str(pdir) if pdir else None,
        "tickets_live": [ticket_packet(t, full=False) for t in live],
        "focus": focus,
        "repos": repos,
        "read_next": {
            "one ticket in full": "atlas context <TICKET-ID>",
            "every ticket": "atlas tickets list",
            "why it is built this way": (str(pdir / "context/state.md") if pdir else None),
            "where it is going": (str(pdir / "context/roadmap.md") if pdir else None),
        },
    }
    packet["packet"] = {"schema": PACKET_SCHEMA, "source": "derived", "mode": "full"}
    if query:
        retrieve(packet, query)
    return compact_packet(packet, budget_chars)


def render(p):
    lines = []
    lines.append(f"project   {p['project'] or '(cwd is not inside a registered project)'}")
    for r in p["repos"]:
        warn = "  ⚠ has a remote" if r["remotes"] else ""
        lines.append(f"git       {r['branch']}  {r['head'][:60]}"
                     f"  ·  {r['uncommitted']} uncommitted{warn}")
    if p["tickets_live"]:
        lines.append("")
        lines.append(f"live tickets ({len(p['tickets_live'])})")
        for t in p["tickets_live"]:
            mark = "→" if (p["focus"] and t["id"] == p["focus"]["id"]) else " "
            lines.append(f"  {mark} {t['id']:<10} {t['state']:<8} {t['title']}")
            if t.get("next_action"):
                lines.append(f"    {'':<10} next: {t['next_action'][:100]}")
            if t.get("blockers"):
                lines.append(f"    {'':<10} BLOCKED: {t['blockers'][:100]}")
    else:
        lines.append("\nlive tickets  none")

    f = p["focus"]
    if f:
        lines.append("")
        lines.append(f"── {f['id']}  {f['state']}  ·  {f['title']}")
        if f.get("class"):
            lines.append(f"   class {f['class']}"
                         + (f" · expected context {f['expected_context']}"
                            if f.get("expected_context") else ""))
        intent = [x for x in (f.get("relation"), f.get("parent"), f.get("requirement"),
                              f.get("goal")) if x]
        if intent:
            lines.append(f"   intent {' · '.join(intent)}")
        if f.get("objective"):
            lines.append(f"\n   objective\n" + indent(f["objective"]))
        lines.append(f"\n   next action\n" + indent(f["next_action"]))
        if f.get("verification"):
            lines.append(f"\n   verification\n" + indent(f["verification"]))
        if f.get("blockers"):
            lines.append(f"\n   blockers\n" + indent(f["blockers"]))
        if f.get("recent_log"):
            lines.append("\n   recent")
            for e in f["recent_log"]:
                lines.append(indent(e.split("\n")[0][:110]))
        lines.append(f"\n   record   {f['record']}")
        if f.get("artifacts"):
            lines.append(f"   beside it   {', '.join(f['artifacts'])}")

    lines.append("\nread next, only if the work needs it")
    for k, v in p["read_next"].items():
        if v:
            lines.append(f"  {k:<26} {v}")
    return "\n".join(lines)


def indent(text, pad="     "):
    return "\n".join(pad + l for l in str(text).strip().splitlines())


def render_boundary(p):
    lines = ["boundary signals — evidence, not a verdict", ""]
    lines.append(f"  project resolved from cwd   {p['project'] or 'none'}")
    lines.append(f"  live tickets in this project {len(p['tickets_live'])}"
                 + (f"  ({', '.join(t['id'] for t in p['tickets_live'])})"
                    if p["tickets_live"] else ""))
    for r in p["repos"]:
        lines.append(f"  uncommitted work            {r['uncommitted']} file(s) on "
                     f"{r['branch']}")
    # What it would cost to come back cold. A boundary is only cheap to cross when the
    # record can rebuild the work without the transcript, so that is reported here
    # rather than left to be assumed.
    ready = [t["id"] for t in p["tickets_live"] if t.get("reconstructs")]
    thin = [t["id"] for t in p["tickets_live"] if not t.get("reconstructs")]
    lines.append(f"  reconstructs cold           {', '.join(ready) if ready else 'none'}")
    if thin:
        lines.append(f"  would re-derive             {', '.join(thin)}"
                     f"  (missing an objective, a next action or verification)")
    lines.append(f"  packet size                 {len(render(p)):,} chars")
    lines.append("")
    lines.append("  Whether the next request continues one of these tickets or starts")
    lines.append("  something else is a reading of intent. This command does not make it,")
    lines.append("  and no keyword rule here would be trustworthy enough to.")
    lines.append("  `atlas lifecycle` turns these signals into a decision, and takes the")
    lines.append("  reading of intent as an explicit flag rather than guessing it.")
    return "\n".join(lines)


def render_planning(p):
    """The same packet, reorganized for planning: evidence only, nothing ranked.

    Fields: project, existing git state, and per ticket — id, state, title, next
    action (first line), blockers if any — plus read-next pointers. Deliberately
    omits objective/verification prose, the recent log and artifacts: those are
    reachable in one named step (`atlas context <ID>`) and are not what planning
    needs. Order is whatever build() already returned — no priority judgement is
    made here.
    """
    lines = [f"project   {p['project'] or '(cwd is not inside a registered project)'}"]
    for r in p["repos"]:
        warn = "  ⚠ has a remote" if r["remotes"] else ""
        lines.append(f"git       {r['branch']}  {r['head'][:60]}"
                     f"  ·  {r['uncommitted']} uncommitted{warn}")

    tickets = list(p["tickets_live"])
    focus_id = p["focus"]["id"] if p["focus"] else None
    if p["focus"] and not any(t["id"] == focus_id for t in tickets):
        tickets.append(p["focus"])  # e.g. an archived ticket looked up directly

    lines.append("")
    if tickets:
        lines.append(f"tickets ({len(tickets)})")
        for t in tickets:
            mark = "→" if t["id"] == focus_id else " "
            lines.append(f"  {mark} {t['id']:<10} {t['state']:<8} {t['title']}")
            na = first_line(t.get("next_action") or "") or "(none recorded)"
            lines.append(f"    {'':<10} next: {na[:100]}")
            if t.get("blockers"):
                lines.append(f"    {'':<10} blocked: {t['blockers'][:100]}")
    else:
        lines.append("tickets   none")

    lines.append("\nread next")
    for k, v in p["read_next"].items():
        if v:
            lines.append(f"  {k:<26} {v}")
    return "\n".join(lines)


def scoped_project_tickets(cwd):
    """Every ticket record (any state, any generation) scoped to the active project the
    same way `build()` scopes its live set: tickets under the resolved project directory,
    falling back to every ticket when the cwd resolves to no project or that project has
    none of its own — so a new session run from outside a registered project's tree still
    gets a recommendation instead of a silent, misleading empty one. Deliberately not
    filtered to `LIVE` here (unlike `build()`'s own `live`): the Smart Dynamic Ticket
    System's candidate set is `atlas_tickets.CANDIDATE_STATES` (todo/active/paused/blocked)
    — `classify_and_rank` applies that filter itself, once, so this and `atlas tickets
    next` can never disagree about which tickets are even in scope.
    """
    projects_root = private_path_or_die("projects")
    tickets = discover(projects_root)
    proj = active_project(projects_root, cwd)
    pdir = project_dir_for(projects_root, proj)
    if pdir:
        scoped = [t for t in tickets if t["project_dir"] == pdir.name]
        return scoped or tickets
    return tickets


def cmd_next(cwd, goal, limit, why, as_json):
    """`--mode next` — the same deterministic recommendation `atlas tickets next`
    produces, from the one shared `atlas_tickets.classify_and_rank`/`render_recommendation`
    implementation (see their own docstrings for the bucket rules). This function selects
    the ticket scope and renders; it computes nothing about priority or bucket itself."""
    buckets = classify_and_rank(scoped_project_tickets(cwd), goal=goal)
    if limit:
        buckets = {k: v[:limit] for k, v in buckets.items()}
    if as_json:
        return json.dumps(buckets, indent=2)
    return render_recommendation(buckets, why=why)


POINTER_REQUIRED = ("ticket", "checkpointed_at", "status")
POINTER_STATUSES = ("pending", "consumed")
POINTER_FIELDS = ("ticket", "checkpointed_at", "state", "next_action", "blockers",
                  "read_with", "status")


def atlas_home():
    return Path(os.environ["ATLAS_HOME"]) if os.environ.get("ATLAS_HOME") \
        else Path.home() / "atlas"


def pointer_path():
    """${ATLAS_HOME:-~/atlas}/runtime/session-handoffs/latest.md — the same env-anchored
    resolution `atlas-observe` already uses (T-015), and that `atlas-tickets`' own writer
    uses on the other end (see its `atlas_runtime_dir()` docstring for why this is
    deliberately NOT derived from wherever the `projects` path resolver happens to
    resolve: `~/atlas/core/cli/atlas-paths`, the copy this file's own `atlas_paths` import
    resolves against, was found to be a stale, pre-T-030 copy that disagrees with the
    canonical resolver on where `projects` even is — verified directly, not assumed).
    Both ends of this pointer read the one same environment convention instead.
    """
    return atlas_home() / "runtime" / "session-handoffs" / "latest.md"


def pointer_field(text, name):
    m = re.search(rf"^{re.escape(name)}:[ \t]*(.*)$", text, re.M)
    return m.group(1).strip() if m else None


def parse_pointer(text):
    """Every declared field, or a ValueError naming exactly what is wrong.

    Deliberately strict: a pointer this command cannot fully make sense of is reported as
    malformed rather than partially trusted. Nothing here mutates the file — a caller that
    catches the error is the one deciding whether `pending` stays untouched.
    """
    fields = {name: pointer_field(text, name) for name in POINTER_FIELDS}
    missing = [n for n in POINTER_REQUIRED if not fields.get(n)]
    if missing:
        raise ValueError(f"missing required field(s): {', '.join(missing)}")
    if fields["status"] not in POINTER_STATUSES:
        raise ValueError(f"status '{fields['status']}' is not one of "
                         f"{'/'.join(POINTER_STATUSES)}")
    return fields


def render_resume(fields):
    lines = [f"pending handoff — {fields['ticket']}",
             f"  checkpointed  {fields['checkpointed_at']}"]
    if fields.get("state"):
        lines.append(f"  state         {fields['state']}")
    if fields.get("next_action"):
        lines.append(f"  next action   {fields['next_action']}")
    if fields.get("blockers"):
        lines.append(f"  blockers      {fields['blockers']}")
    lines.append(f"  read with     {fields.get('read_with') or 'atlas context ' + fields['ticket']}")
    return "\n".join(lines)


def mark_consumed(path, expect):
    """Flip status -> consumed, but only if the file is still the exact pointer just
    rendered (same ticket, same checkpoint time, still pending) — a concurrent new
    checkpoint between render and this call must be left alone, still pending, untouched.
    Best-effort: a failure here does not undo the successful render already printed.
    """
    try:
        fresh = path.read_text()
    except OSError as e:
        print(f"atlas context --resume: could not confirm pointer before marking "
              f"consumed: {e}", file=sys.stderr)
        return
    if (pointer_field(fresh, "ticket") != expect["ticket"]
            or pointer_field(fresh, "checkpointed_at") != expect["checkpointed_at"]
            or pointer_field(fresh, "status") != "pending"):
        return  # superseded by a newer checkpoint, or already changed — leave it be
    updated = re.sub(r"^status:[ \t]*.*$", "status: consumed", fresh, count=1, flags=re.M)
    try:
        tmp = path.with_name(path.name + f".tmp{os.getpid()}")
        tmp.write_text(updated)
        os.replace(tmp, path)
    except OSError as e:
        print(f"atlas context --resume: pointer shown but not marked consumed "
              f"({e}) — it may be shown again", file=sys.stderr)


def cmd_resume(as_json):
    """Read-only discovery of the latest checkpoint pointer. No project scan, no git call,
    no file read beyond this one pointer. Never executes anything named inside it."""
    path = pointer_path()
    if not path.is_file():
        print(json.dumps({"pending": False}) if as_json else "no pending handoff")
        return 0

    try:
        text = path.read_text()
    except OSError as e:
        print(f"atlas context --resume: could not read {path}: {e}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 3

    try:
        fields = parse_pointer(text)
    except ValueError as e:
        print(f"atlas context --resume: malformed pointer at {path}: {e}", file=sys.stderr)
        return 3  # left exactly as found — no status was touched

    if fields["status"] == "consumed":
        print(json.dumps({"pending": False}) if as_json else "no pending handoff")
        return 0

    try:
        rendered = json.dumps({"pending": True, **fields}) if as_json else render_resume(fields)
    except Exception as e:
        print(f"atlas context --resume: failed to render pending handoff: {e}",
              file=sys.stderr)
        return 3  # rendering failed — pending is preserved, nothing was consumed
    print(rendered)
    mark_consumed(path, fields)
    return 0


def main(argv):
    ap = argparse.ArgumentParser(prog="atlas context")
    ap.add_argument("ticket", nargs="?")
    ap.add_argument("--json", action="store_true")
    ap.add_argument("--goal", help="only meaningful with --mode next")
    ap.add_argument("--limit", type=int, help="only meaningful with --mode next")
    ap.add_argument("--why", action="store_true", help="only meaningful with --mode next")
    ap.add_argument("--query", help="only with the default/--json/--boundary packet: "
                    "rank tickets_live by relevance, record the match in packet['retrieval']")
    ap.add_argument("--budget-chars", type=int,
                    help="only with the default/--json/--boundary packet: compact the "
                    "JSON packet to this many characters")
    group = ap.add_mutually_exclusive_group()
    group.add_argument("--boundary", action="store_true")
    group.add_argument("--mode", choices=["planning", "next"])
    group.add_argument("--resume", action="store_true")
    a = ap.parse_args(argv)
    # --mode planning is a distinct, non-composable view: silently letting --json win here
    # would be exactly the hidden behavior this flag exists to avoid. --mode next is not
    # under the same restriction — it composes with --json on purpose, per its own
    # machine-readable output requirement — so this check names 'planning' specifically
    # rather than every --mode value.
    if a.mode == "planning" and a.json:
        ap.error("argument --json: not allowed with --mode planning")
    if (a.goal or a.limit is not None or a.why) and a.mode != "next":
        ap.error("--goal/--limit/--why require --mode next")
    if (a.query or a.budget_chars is not None) and (a.mode or a.resume):
        ap.error("--query/--budget-chars are not allowed with --mode or --resume")
    if a.resume:
        # A positional ticket makes no sense with --resume (the pointer names its own
        # ticket) and is silently ignored rather than rejected — a low-risk, non-
        # destructive combination not worth a hard error.
        return cmd_resume(a.json)
    if a.mode == "next":
        print(cmd_next(os.getcwd(), a.goal, a.limit, a.why, a.json))
        return 0
    try:
        p = build(os.getcwd(), a.ticket, a.query, a.budget_chars)
    except ValueError as e:
        ap.error(str(e))
    if a.mode == "planning":
        print(render_planning(p))
    elif a.json:
        print(json.dumps(p, indent=2))
    elif a.boundary:
        print(render_boundary(p))
    else:
        print(render(p))
    return 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main(sys.argv[1:]))
